<%# The app-ladder row for /deployments: one CI testing card per reportable repo,
    showing where each application's suite sits on `accepted → release → main`.

    WHY IT EXISTS. An application's progress along the ladder is decoupled from the
    Next Release card. Work sits merged on `accepted` for hours before a sweep
    promotes it, and between releases Next Release reads "none active" while several
    repos quietly hold unshipped work. That in-between state had no surface at all.

    ONE ROW THAT SCROLLS, not a wrapping grid. The grid put the fifth repo on a second
    line under three empty columns, so the row's height grew with the ecosystem and
    the board it sits above was pushed down for every app onboarded. A single row
    keeps that cost flat: past the fourth card the row scrolls sideways, and the FADE
    at its right edge is what says there is more — a hard clipped edge reads as the
    end of the list, which is the one thing it must not say.

    THE FADE IS A MASK ON THE ROW ITSELF, not a gradient laid over it — the same
    vocabulary components/_overflow_fade and the CI meter's mark row already use here.
    An overlay must be painted in the page's own background colour, which is a claim
    about what sits behind the cards; a mask makes the content itself fall away, so it
    is right in both themes over any surface, with no second colour to keep in step.

    AND IT IS MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED. It shows while the scroller actually has content
    to the right, and clears the moment you reach the end — seeded server-side from the
    card count so the first paint is right before Alpine measures anything. A fade over
    a row with nothing behind it is a promise of content that does not exist.

    THE PINNED STRIP is the second half of the ask: scroll past this row and the same
    applications re-appear fixed under the site header, condensed to three rows —
    name, CI meter, ladder (tasks/_app_ladder_pinned_card). It is rendered HERE, inside
    #app-ladder-row, on purpose: the broadcast below replaces this slot, so the strip
    refreshes with the row and can never sit pinned to the top of the page showing a
    verdict the row beneath it has already moved past.

    #app-ladder-row IS A STABLE SLOT, and that is load-bearing rather than cosmetic.
    The board takes live updates over Turbo Streams (`turbo_stream_from "deployments"`),
    and a stream can only replace a target it can name. Without this id the row was
    invisible to DeploymentsBroadcaster: the dev deploy tools (Open / Advance / Ship /
    Redraw / Reset) POST to /dev/board/<action>, which fires .release_modules, which
    replaces #current-release and #last-release — and nothing re-rendered this row, so
    it sat stale until a manual reload while every other card moved. The id plus
    DeploymentsBroadcaster.app_ladder is the fix; keep them in step.

    Locals: cards - Array<Ci::AppLadder::Card>, already sorted worst-first. %>
<% cards = local_assigns.fetch(:cards, []) %>
<%# The fade's FIRST-PAINT seed. Ci::AppLadder::ROW_FADE_AT is the count that cannot
    fit the board's widest container, so it is the one thing knowable about overflow
    before a browser has measured; from init on, the browser's own measurement rules. %>
<% seed_fade = cards.size >= Ci::AppLadder::ROW_FADE_AT %>
<%# data-rendered-at is the row's own render stamp, and it is diagnostic rather than
    decorative: it answers "when was this row last refreshed" for anyone looking at a
    board they suspect is stale — the precise question the missing broadcast made
    unanswerable. It is also what makes the wiring guard DETERMINISTIC. Asserting that
    the row's CONTENT changed is flaky by construction: opening a fixture release does
    not necessarily move any rung verdict or parked count, so an identical re-render is
    a legitimate outcome and the test would fail on a wiring that works. The stamp
    changes on every render, so it proves the MECHANISM — a broadcast arrived and
    replaced this slot — independent of whether the content happened to differ. %>
<div id="app-ladder-row" class="mb-6" data-test="app-ladder-row"
     data-rendered-at="<%= (Time.current.to_f * 1000).to_i %>">
  <% if cards.any? %>
    <%# THE CONTROLLER, inline. No <script> tag: this fragment is replaced by a Turbo
        Stream broadcast, and Alpine re-initialises replaced DOM on its own while a
        script inside a replaced fragment is a re-execution question nobody should have
        to answer on a live board.

        `offset` is MEASURED off the site header rather than hard-coded, because that
        header shrinks on scroll (py-6 → py-2 in layouts/application) and a fixed strip
        pinned to a stale number would either float in a gap or slide under the nav.
        Measuring also means a banner in studio/banners/stack — which pushes the header
        down — costs nothing here.

        AND IT IS A ResizeObserver, NOT ONLY A SCROLL HANDLER, for the reason
        components/_overflow_fade learned the hard way: a scroll is one CAUSE of the
        header's box changing, the observer watches the EFFECT. Measured here at 8px —
        the header's height TRANSITIONS over 300ms (`transition-all duration-300`), so
        the last scroll event fires while it is still mid-animation and every reading
        after that is stale. The strip then sits in a visible gap under a nav that
        finished shrinking without telling anybody. The same observer covers the fade's
        two scrollers, so a card widening or a font swap re-measures too.

        The observer belongs to the instance and is released in destroy(): this row is
        REPLACED wholesale by a Turbo Stream broadcast, so one left behind would
        accumulate per broadcast on a board that broadcasts all day. %>
    <div x-data="{
           fadeRight: '<%= ApplicationHelper::APP_LADDER_FADE_MASK %>',
           pinned: false,
           offset: 0,
           overflowing: <%= seed_fade %>,
           atEnd: false,
           pinnedOverflowing: <%= seed_fade %>,
           pinnedAtEnd: false,
           _ro: null,
           _dead: false,
           init() {
             this.$nextTick(() => {
               if (this._dead) return;
               this.measure();
               if (!window.ResizeObserver) return;
               this._ro = new ResizeObserver(() => this.measure());
               const header = document.querySelector('.vt-pinned-header');
               if (header) this._ro.observe(header);
               if (this.$refs.scroller) this._ro.observe(this.$refs.scroller);
               if (this.$refs.pinnedScroller) this._ro.observe(this.$refs.pinnedScroller);
             });
           },
           destroy() {
             this._dead = true;
             if (this._ro) { this._ro.disconnect(); this._ro = null; }
           },
           measure() {
             const header = document.querySelector('.vt-pinned-header');
             this.offset = header ? Math.round(header.getBoundingClientRect().bottom) : 0;
             const row = this.$refs.row;
             this.pinned = !!row && row.getBoundingClientRect().bottom <= this.offset;
             this.gauge();
           },
           gauge() {
             const el = this.$refs.scroller;
             if (el) {
               this.overflowing = el.scrollWidth - el.clientWidth > 8;
               this.atEnd = el.scrollLeft + el.clientWidth >= el.scrollWidth - 8;
             }
             const strip = this.$refs.pinnedScroller;
             if (strip) {
               this.pinnedOverflowing = strip.scrollWidth - strip.clientWidth > 8;
               this.pinnedAtEnd = strip.scrollLeft + strip.clientWidth >= strip.scrollWidth - 8;
             }
           }
         }"
         @scroll.window="measure()"
         @resize.window="measure()">
      <div class="flex items-baseline gap-2 mb-2">
        <h2 class="text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted">Applications</h2>
        <span class="text-[10px] text-muted"><%= pluralize(cards.count, "repo") %> on the ladder</span>
      </div>

      <%# x-ref="row" is what "have we scrolled past the applications" is measured
          against — the wrapper rather than the scroller, so the reading survives any
          padding the scroller grows for its scrollbar. %>
      <div class="min-w-0" x-ref="row">
        <%# scrollbar-thin/-none is not in play here — the row keeps its native
            scrollbar so a trackpad-less operator can still drag it. %>
        <div class="flex min-w-0 items-stretch gap-4 overflow-x-auto pb-1"
             data-test="app-ladder-scroller"
             x-ref="scroller"
             <%# Seeded server-side so the first paint is already right; Alpine's own
                 binding takes the attribute over on init and clears it at the end of
                 the scroll. %>
             <%= tag.attributes(data: { faded: true }, style: ApplicationHelper::APP_LADDER_FADE_MASK) if seed_fade %>
             :data-faded="overflowing && !atEnd"
             :style="(overflowing && !atEnd) ? fadeRight : ''"
             @scroll="gauge()">
          <% cards.each do |card| %>
            <%# FULLY QUALIFIED on purpose. This partial is rendered two ways: inline from
                TasksController (where a bare "app_ladder_card" resolves against tasks/)
                AND standalone by DeploymentsBroadcaster via ApplicationController.render,
                where the same bare name resolves against application/ and raises Missing
                partial. Studio::Cable.safe_broadcast SWALLOWS that — so the broadcast
                failed silently and the row simply never moved, with nothing in the log to
                say why. tasks/_current_release carries the same fully-qualified habit. %>
            <%= render "tasks/app_ladder_card", card: card, card_class: "w-80 shrink-0" %>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      </div>

      <%# THE PINNED STRIP. Fixed under the site header (z-40 — the header is z-50 and
          must always win), full-bleed so it reads as page chrome, and carrying the same
          container gutters as the board so the tiles line up with the cards they came
          from. `style="display: none"` is the no-JS/pre-init state: nothing is pinned
          until something has measured that the row has actually left the screen. %>
      <%# `:style` BINDS AN OBJECT, and that is a correctness rule rather than a taste
          one. Alpine's STRING form of :style calls setAttribute("style", …), which
          replaces the whole attribute — including the `display: none` that x-show wrote
          — and `offset` changes on every scroll while the header shrinks. So the string
          form would unhide this strip over the top of the board the moment the operator
          scrolled with the row still on screen. The object form sets one property and
          leaves x-show's alone. %>
      <div class="fixed inset-x-0 z-40 border-b border-subtle bg-page/95 shadow-lg supports-[backdrop-filter]:backdrop-blur"
           data-test="app-ladder-pinned"
           role="region"
           aria-label="Applications, pinned to the top of the page"
           x-show="pinned"
           :style="{ top: offset + 'px' }"
           style="display: none"
           x-transition.opacity.duration.150ms>
        <div class="mx-auto w-full max-w-screen-2xl px-3 py-1.5 sm:px-4 lg:px-6 xl:px-8">
            <div class="flex min-w-0 items-stretch gap-2 overflow-x-auto"
                 data-test="app-ladder-pinned-scroller"
                 x-ref="pinnedScroller"
                 <%= tag.attributes(data: { faded: true }, style: ApplicationHelper::APP_LADDER_FADE_MASK) if seed_fade %>
                 :data-faded="pinnedOverflowing && !pinnedAtEnd"
                 :style="(pinnedOverflowing && !pinnedAtEnd) ? fadeRight : ''"
                 @scroll="gauge()">
              <% cards.each do |card| %>
                <%= render "tasks/app_ladder_pinned_card", card: card %>
              <% end %>
            </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
